Consider a semiclassical black hole of initial ADM mass $M_{BH}$ that undergoes Hawking evaporation. In a "remnant" scenario, evaporation does not proceed to complete evanescence: it halts either when the mass approaches the Planck mass $m_P=\sqrt{\hbar c/G}$, or when it approaches some intermediate remnant mass scale $M_r$ with $m_P \ll M_r \ll M_{BH}$, leaving behind an eternal or extremely long-lived localized object. The remnant is required to:

(i) preserve unitarity of the full evaporation process by either storing all of the information that fell into the original black hole or releasing it on arbitrarily long timescales;
(ii) shield the curvature singularity from external observers, so that no naked singularity is seen at any stage of evaporation, including near $m_P$;
(iii) carry an entropy at least equal to the initial Bekenstein–Hawking entropy $S_{BH}=A/(4\ell_P^2)$ of the original black hole, while having a localized spatial extent of order at most the Planck (or remnant) length.

Determine whether there exists a consistent semiclassical/quantum-gravitational model of such a remnant which simultaneously satisfies (i)–(iii) without:

(a) violating the Bekenstein entropy bound $S \le 2\pi k_B R E/(\hbar c)$ for a system of effective energy $E$ and radius $R$;
(b) producing pathologies such as an infinite species problem or unbounded pair-production of remnants in low-energy processes;
(c) conflicting with the holographic counting of microstates expected from the AdS/CFT correspondence for asymptotically AdS black holes.

Either construct such a model and prove its consistency with (a)–(c), or prove that no such remnant model can exist.

